//!
//! # Context Switching
//!
//! The machine-specific part of a kernel thread: a stack switch.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! A thread that is not running is a stack pointer. `Arch::Context::switchTo`
//! stores the callee-saved registers and RSP of the running code and resumes
//! another stack pointer, and `Arch::Context::prepareStack` builds the stack of a
//! thread that has never run so that the first switch into it starts its
//! function. The layout and the alignment rule are described in
//! `context_asm.S`.
//!
//! Switching must happen with interrupts disabled, and `switchTo` panics when
//! they are not. Interrupt-disabled sections are also the only mutual
//! exclusion the kernel has: it runs on one processor, so nothing else can
//! touch the data while interrupts are off.
//!
//! ## What a Switch Does
//!
//! ```text
//!   thread A calls Arch::Context::switchTo(&A.savedRsp, B.savedRsp)
//!
//!   A's stack                              B's stack
//!   ┌────────────────┐                     ┌────────────────┐
//!   │ ... A's frames │                     │ ... B's frames │
//!   │ return address │                     │ return address │ <- B's resume point
//!   │ rbp rbx r12-r15│ <- saved here       │ rbp rbx r12-r15│ <- loaded from here
//!   └───────▲────────┘                     └───────▲────────┘
//!           │ A.savedRsp := rsp                    │ rsp := B.savedRsp
//!
//!   then: pop r15..rbp, ret   ->   B continues where it called switchTo
//! ```
//!
//! A switch is an ordinary function call as far as the compiler is concerned,
//! so only the registers the ABI says a callee must preserve need saving.
//!
//! ## A New Thread
//!
//! `prepareStack` writes seven words at the top of the stack. T is the 16-byte
//! aligned end of the stack and `entry` the thread function:
//!
//! ```text
//!   address   value                        popped into / used as
//!   T - 56    0                            r15
//!   T - 48    0                            r14
//!   T - 40    argument                     r13  -> rdi when the function is called
//!   T - 32    entry                        r12  -> "call r12" in the trampoline
//!   T - 24    0                            rbx
//!   T - 16    0                            rbp  (ends frame-pointer walks)
//!   T -  8    ignis_thread_trampoline      return address of the first switch
//!   T         <- RSP while the trampoline runs
//! ```
//!
//! The trampoline calls `Sched::Scheduler::threadBegin`, enables interrupts, calls
//! `entry(argument)` and, when it returns, calls `Sched::Scheduler::exitCurrent`.
//! See concept doc 13.

import _ from "./cpu";

namespace Arch {
  /// Functions implemented in `context_asm.S`.
  extern ContextAsm {
    /// Saves the callee-saved registers on the current stack, stores RSP in
    /// `*saveRsp`, loads `nextRsp` and resumes that context. Interrupts must be
    /// off.
    function ignis_switch_context(saveRsp: *mut u64, nextRsp: u64): void;

    /// The address of the code every new thread starts in.
    function ignis_thread_trampoline_address(): u64;

    /// The address of a thread function. It has to be called with the function
    /// named at the call site, because an extern function takes only a
    /// capture-free function written there, not a function value passed on.
    function ignis_function_address(entry: (u64) -> void): u64;
  }
}

/// The context switching API.
namespace Arch::Context {
  /// Registers the first switch pops, plus the return address it jumps to.
  const INITIAL_SLOTS: u64 = 7;

  /// Index of the slot (from the lowest address) that holds r13, the argument.
  const ARGUMENT_SLOT: u64 = 2;

  /// Index of the slot that holds r12, the thread function.
  const FUNCTION_SLOT: u64 = 3;

  /// Index of the slot that holds the return address, the trampoline.
  const RETURN_SLOT: u64 = 6;

  /// Stores the running context in `*saveRsp` and continues the one saved as
  /// `nextRsp`. Returns when some other context switches back to the stored
  /// value. Interrupts must be disabled.
  function switchTo(saveRsp: *mut u64, nextRsp: u64): void {
    if (Arch::Cpu::interruptsEnabled()) {
      @panic("context: switch with interrupts enabled");
    }

    Arch::ContextAsm::ignis_switch_context(saveRsp, nextRsp);
  }

  /// Lays out the stack of a thread that has never run and returns the stack
  /// pointer to hand to `switchTo`. `stackEnd` is the address just above the
  /// last byte of the stack and must be 16-byte aligned: the trampoline
  /// starts with RSP equal to it, and its call pushes the return address, so
  /// `entry` starts with RSP + 8 aligned to 16. The thread function receives
  /// `argument` in RDI.
  function prepareStack(stackEnd: u64, entry: u64, argument: u64): u64 {
    if (stackEnd % 16 != 0) {
      @panic("context: the end of a stack must be 16-byte aligned");
    }

    let base: u64 = stackEnd - INITIAL_SLOTS * 8;
    let slots: *mut u64 = base as *mut u64;

    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < INITIAL_SLOTS) {
      @write<u64>(slots + index, 0);
      index += 1;
    }

    @write<u64>(slots + ARGUMENT_SLOT, argument);
    @write<u64>(slots + FUNCTION_SLOT, entry);
    @write<u64>(slots + RETURN_SLOT, Arch::ContextAsm::ignis_thread_trampoline_address());

    return base;
  }
}
